== Release Summary Information

== Required eta-ais version (gundas1)

AIS_APP_TEMPLATE_4.0.0_ALPHA_31

== Add BmiJ1939 app (gundas1)

Added new BmiJ1939 Rx app that is configurable using JSON.
The following Rx services are supported:
 - CDL PID over J1939 (SPB, PGB, Real Poll, Map, ADT)
 - PGN

== Add app registration to BmiCdl and PwmInputApp (gundas1)

Updated BmiCdl and PwmInputApp to support app registration through
the AppRegInput interface.

== Create PartNumbers channel (gundas1)

Removed TES Common version of ShmClock.  The part numbers and product id
were added to a new PartNumbers channel.  Updated VP3FileCreationApp to
use the new PartNumbers channel.

== Add Product ID support (gundas1)

Updated DataLinkData to support the variable length Product ID parameter.

== Add TMAC file support (gundas1)

Updated FileTransferBridgeApp to support TMAC files.

== Fix VP3 header timestamp (gundas1)

Updated VP3FileCreationApp correctly to use the timezone offset provided by ShmClock.
Added get_tz_struct and get_tz_data helper functions to directly get/set timezone
information from/to an ShmClock object.

== Add common NVM store/read functions (gundas1)

Added nvm::store and nvm::read template functions for any
object that implements boost::serialize.  Contained in <fileio/nvm.hpp>

Added directory::create to provide a standard exception handling function for
creating directories.  Contained in <fileio/directory.hpp>

== Change iflocker to use a shared lock (gundas1)

Changed iflocker to use a shared lock for reading instead of an exclusive write lock.

== Add a ISO8601UTC time parsing (gundas1)

Added a ISO8601UTC time parsing utility in print.hpp.

== Add IoTHubComms to file transfer bridge app (gundas1)

Added the IoTHubComms to file transfer bridge app.
NOTE: Commented out the code that started that service.

== Fix remaining bytes to read is decremented twice for a single read (gundas1)

In "boost/cgi/cgi/request_service.hpp" in parse_post_vars(), the remaining bytes
to read is decremented twice for a single read. Once in the client read_some,
and once in the request service parst_post_vars. This is because a reference
to the client bytes_left is used rather than a copy of the bytes left.

std::size_t& bytes_left (impl.client_.bytes_left_);

Should be
std::size_t bytes_left (impl.client_.bytes_left_);

For content lengths that are not multiples of 128, the double decrement rolls
over and the post data is read until the eof is encountered, possibly greater
than the content length.

